g for looker but in case this is  useful to you  looker caches data and that means it's  both faster for the end user and cheaper  because that bigquery of course charges  for storage which generally isn't that  much unless you have all the records  that we have and for query time and so  if you cache and that means you're even  less worried about what Google's  charging you another thing is looker  makes it very easy to build reports in  which you can filter and double-click  and there's something people love about  being able to drill down by  double-clicking they love the drill down  and look I don't know that  makes it it makes it easy people love  that stuff the other thing is whatever  you're looking at it looker with all the  filters and the drill downs and the  things is all in the get URL so you can  just bookmark or throw that URL at  somebody or whatever and they can leap  right into that particular thing and  that's not true of some of these other  tools  that's just so handy and practice to be  able to do that or if you're building  dashboards so you know I I'm an analyst  tonight I have a3 dashboards I build for  my little my function I mean for me to  be able to just say oh I'll have three  tabs and each one is just this URL and  updating what's in it is just updating  the URL how easy is this it's super easy  or people on their laptops could just  bookmark a URL it's just it becomes  really easy to do also all the things  that are in there are in bed Abul so for  example there's a lot of this kind of  data we wanted to put into salesforce  into the UI of salesforce because if  you're in the sales team you live in  salesforce you don't want to live in  salesforce and some reporting tool and  be jumping around that's not good  so with looker we're able to take these  take these reports or charts or whatever  kinds of things and just a little piece  like one chart with this filter on it of  the past week or whenever and you can  embed that right into the dashboard in  salesforce with with no code and  salesforce and no special crap no  special add-ons you can just embed it  again in part because of this deep  linking that goes right in so that means  we're able to build the sales team I  should say the sales ops team is able to  build dashboards in Salesforce for the  reps and the managers they never have to  leave Salesforce to get this data which  is awesome again that's that's looker  but they still have the double-click so  if they go well what about this bullet  now they're now they're in looker and  they can go play so it's just again a  really nice thing for people and these  again as engineers sometimes you think  of this like who cares like so it's  embedded in this dashboard instead of  that winner so what you can click on it  doesn't sound like a big deal but in  practice especially for non-technical  people the idea that it's just there and  I can click and get more is actually a  really big usability factor and that's  what this part of the system should be  about is usability for the folks that  need to use the data so that's that's  what we picked  so a couple of things um it was harder  to it was harder to connect to all the  systems of record than we thought even  with even with things like segments we  actually had to file and get some bugs  fixed from segment just to get our  Salesforce data to work so it wasn't  easy we also ran into some trickiness  with secrets so for example we used  Apache airflow to create a work flow  around around how to pull all this data  so in other words you know pull it from  all these systems and then make these  transformations and then these and an  old series we use the patchy airflow  it's a workflow system for defining  those and doing it once again how do we  create that those rules we've got it  from the JSON and we spit it out and it  goes right into air flow and Google has  a managed air flow by the way which we  use and then it runs those things on  kubernetes so then we were able to use  the kubernetes secrets system to  actually take all those secrets to all  those systems which of course those are  crown jewel secrets so that's really  careful  our financial data our customer data  this is a scary stuff but by being able  to put it into the vault in kubernetes  and letting it run in there it's a nice  safe way where no human being ever gets  their mitts on those secrets and yet you  know we can automate the pulling of all  the data so that's that's really nice so  it was hard to get that to work actually  um it was non-trivial to figure out how  to get multiple things into the vault  how to make sure the kubernetes got the  stuff out of the vault these things were  not easy for us to get up and running at  first but but once they were running  it's now been running for maybe three  and a half months with not one problem  no maintenance issue no no nothing at  all so that's that's really good so so  overall overall it was really good even  though it was a it was a challenge to  get going it's still it's still  difficult to this day to understand what  data is right and I think even though  we've built a lot of systems for that  it's it's still hard so for example  we'll pull data from our general ledger  we'll pull data from stripe where we run  the credit card side of the business and  from the user portal where we show  invoices and then it'll be customers  where that stuff doesn't match you say  okay but all this stuff is supposed to  be right and at that point Google can't  help you and JSON can't help you  so just running through all that has  taken and we continue to work on that  stuff today it has taken a lot more time  than expected then than we expected when  we've talked to other folks even in the  even in the server business about  billing and those kinds of areas we  discovered that Google has this problem  Amazon has this problem  Rackspace had this problem actually  billing turns out to be really hard but  who knew but actually it's really hard  so at least though we have a system  where it's easy for us to understand  what it is that we are doing in the  first place and therefore maybe iterate  and diagnose the other thing we did is  we made test obviously unit test  integration test everybody knows that so  of course we did that the other thing  that we did is these Spock check smoke  tests so what we do is we'd say we  discover some customer with weird data  and so okay what we we would create a  CSV file of the rate of the result of  some report against that customer that  was actually right and that became a  test right so every time we update all  the corporate data we'll run this  particular report and check it against  the test CSV to make sure that we didn't  break that so it's like an integration  test in that it's a end-to-end test but  it's on the actual production data so  it's like a third level of tests we of  course in those we have to be careful  just to read and validate and never  change anything unlike an integration  test or you're allowed to change the  state because you can reset it so by  having just the system and we've  probably all built these kinds of things  where you can drop a CSV file somewhere  and drop a query somewhere and add a  test easily because of that we've been  able to add about a hundred tests which  doesn't sound like a lot but it's you  know we have maybe five or six hundred  unit tests but we were able to add an  end-to-end test so now even when we find  something really strange in boundary and  we make a little change to the JSON  we're very confident after it gets  deployed that we haven't broken anything  in the other crazy cases that we've seen  right so taking the time to build those  in production tests that has been that  has been useful in this other problem so  we've changed we've changed things as a  result these are some of them I'll give  you a funny example you would think that  the number of customer